---
// The explanation below the Lab: the pictures, the symbols, how t-SNE draws
// its map, perplexity, how to read the map, and the model in short. Static
// HTML; formulas are rendered by KaTeX at build time.
import { renderTex } from '../../lib/katex';
import { equations } from './equations';

const tex = Object.fromEntries(equations.map((e) => [e.id, renderTex(e.tex)]));
---

<div class="explain">
  <section aria-labelledby="tsne-data">
    <h2 id="tsne-data">What you are looking at</h2>
    <p>
      Each square on the map is a tiny picture, 8 by 8 pixels. To the computer,
      a picture is just 64 numbers: how dark each pixel is. There are four kinds
      of shape (a bar across, a bar down, a slash and a backslash), drawn at
      slightly different places, with a few smudges.
    </p>
    <p>
      64 numbers cannot be drawn on a flat page. t-SNE draws a map instead: it
      places the pictures so that pictures with similar pixels end up close
      together. It is never told the kinds; the coloured frames are only there
      for you to check its work. The pictures are made up, and{' '}
      <strong>Refresh Data</strong> shows another set.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="tsne-symbols">
    <h2 id="tsne-symbols">What each symbol means</h2>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <div>
        <dt>xᵢ</dt>
        <dd>Picture i as its 64 pixel numbers.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>yᵢ</dt>
        <dd>Where picture i sits on the map.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>pᵢⱼ</dt>
        <dd>How close pictures i and j are in pixels, as a probability.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>qᵢⱼ</dt>
        <dd>How close they are on the map, measured the same way.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>σᵢ</dt>
        <dd>The width of picture i's neighbourhood in pixels.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-a">u</dt>
        <dd>
          The perplexity: roughly how many neighbours each picture pays
          attention to.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-line">t</dt>
        <dd>How many steps the map has moved so far.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="tsne-how">
    <h2 id="tsne-how">How t-SNE draws its map</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Measure closeness in the pictures.</strong> For each picture,
        put a bell curve over the pixel distances to all the others. Near
        pictures get a high probability, far ones almost none. Each curve's
        width σᵢ is chosen so that it covers about u neighbours.
        <div class="formula" set:html={tex.p} />
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Measure closeness on the map</strong> in the same way, but with
        a curve that has longer tails. That gives far-apart groups room to
        spread out instead of piling up in the middle.
        <div class="formula" set:html={tex.q} />
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Move the map</strong> a little at a time to make the two match.
        The mismatch is measured by the KL cost; each step moves every picture
        downhill on it. Pairs that should be close pull together; pairs that
        should be apart push away.
        <div class="formula" set:html={tex.cost} />
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The map starts as a tiny random cloud. For the first 250 steps the pulls
      are made 12 times stronger, so groups form early and can then settle into
      place.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="tsne-perplexity">
    <h2 id="tsne-perplexity">Perplexity</h2>
    <p>
      With a small perplexity, each picture cares only about its very nearest
      few, so the map shows small, tight clumps, sometimes splitting a kind into
      pieces. With a larger perplexity, each picture looks further, and the map
      shows the bigger groups more clearly. Typical values are between 5 and 50;
      it can never be more than the number of other pictures.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="tsne-read">
    <h2 id="tsne-read">How to read the map</h2>
    <p>
      Closeness on the map is meaningful: near pictures are similar. But sizes
      and gaps are not. A tight group is not necessarily more alike than a loose
      one, and the distance between two groups says little about how different
      they are. The map also has no up or down: run it again and the groups may
      come out turned or mirrored.
    </p>
    <p>
      The score <strong>Neighbours kept</strong> checks the map honestly: for
      each picture, how many of its 5 nearest pictures in pixels are also among
      its 5 nearest on the map.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="tsne-summary">
    <h2 id="tsne-summary">The model in short</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Measure</strong> how close every pair is in the data.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Start</strong> with a tiny random map.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Move</strong> the map step by step until closeness on the map
        matches closeness in the data.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Read</strong> who is near whom, not the sizes or the gaps.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</div>

<style>
  .explain {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-12);
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: var(--fs-16);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  section {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-4);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: var(--fs-24);
  }

  .formula {
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding-block: var(--s-2);
    font-size: var(--fs-18);
  }

  .symbols {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .symbols div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 5rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-4);
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  .symbols dt {
    font-family: 'KaTeX_Math', 'Times New Roman', serif;
    font-size: var(--fs-20);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .symbols dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .steps {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
    padding-inline-start: 1.4em;
  }
</style>
